//============================================================================
// nanosoc_eth_chiplet_pads --- Calibre DRC wrapper deck   (PROJECT-OWNED)
//
// *** NO LONGER THE DEFAULT DECK. ***  As of 2026-08-10 run_drc.sh assembles the
// PROJECT deck (tsmc65_minisic_header.svrf + the foundry rule bodies, spliced by
// make_project_deck.sh) because this wrapper CANNOT clear the WLCSP seal-ring
// switch or set the chip-window bounds --- see "CANNOT BE SET FROM THIS WRAPPER"
// below. This file is kept as the A/B control: it reproduces the deck with the
// FOUNDRY switch defaults, which is the only way to measure what the project
// header changed.
//
// Run it with:   DRC_DECK=$PWD/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_pads.drc.rules \
//                ASIC/genus-innovus/scripts/calibre/run_drc.sh
// or by hand:    cd <rundir> && DRC_GDS=/abs/path/to.gds \
//                DRC_FOUNDRY_DECK=/abs/path/to/foundry/deck \
//                calibre -drc -hier -turbo 8 -nowait <this file> < /dev/null
//
// Measured 2026-08-10 on runs/20260810T065131Z_honest-full-pnr2:
//   this wrapper (foundry defaults) : 1955 rulechecks, 2509 results
//   project deck (header landed)    : 1926 rulechecks, 2508 results
//   the whole delta is the WLCSP -> wire-bond branch swap; the only non-zero
//   check that moves is AP.R.1, 1 -> gone.
//
// WHICH FOUNDRY DECK: this file does not name one. $DRC_FOUNDRY_DECK is resolved
// per site from $(TSMC_65_HOME) by ASIC/genus-innovus/drc_project.mk, which is
// gitignored. The deck's release name encodes a metal stack and a purchased
// revision, so it is site configuration, not source. See ci/site.env.example.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
// --------------------
// The TSMC deck ships with *placeholder* specification statements that the
// customer is expected to replace: the layout path, the primary cell, and the
// results-database and summary-report filenames are literal strings, not
// variables. The deck's own text is not reproduced here -- this repository is
// public and TSMC's licence does not permit it. Read the block in your own PDK.
//
// Pointing Calibre straight at the foundry deck therefore fails: Calibre looks
// for a file *named* by the placeholder in the current directory. It is not a
// variable and nothing in Innovus, Tcl or the shell substitutes it. (Setting a
// same-spelled variable in a Tcl script sets a *Tcl* variable and has no effect
// whatsoever on the SVRF deck.)
//
// THE LOAD-BEARING LINE IS `DRC ICSTATION YES` -- DO NOT REMOVE IT
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// SVRF normally rejects a specification statement that is given twice (error
// SPC1, "superfluous specification statement"), so simply setting LAYOUT PRIMARY
// here and then INCLUDEing the foundry deck (which sets it again) is a hard
// error -- verified on this site, both orderings, with and without -turbo.
//
// `DRC ICSTATION YES` switches Calibre into the mode Calibre Interactive uses:
// duplicate specification statements are tolerated and the ones in the
// TOP-LEVEL rule file win. That is what makes this wrapper pattern legal.
// Verified 2026-08-06 on Calibre v2023.1_18.8: removing this one line turns a
// clean run back into SPC1.
//
// Everything below the INCLUDE is the unmodified foundry deck. The foundry
// deck is READ-ONLY (the PDK mount is shared, lab-wide collateral) and is not
// copied, patched or sed-ed by this flow.
//============================================================================

// ---- must come first: enables top-level override of the deck's placeholders
DRC ICSTATION YES

// ---- input layout -----------------------------------------------------------
// $DRC_GDS is expanded by Calibre itself. SVRF expands environment variables in
// *file path* strings (LAYOUT PATH, INCLUDE, DRC RESULTS DATABASE) but NOT in
// cell-name strings -- so LAYOUT PRIMARY below must stay literal. Verified.
LAYOUT PATH    "$DRC_GDS"
LAYOUT SYSTEM  GDSII
LAYOUT PRIMARY "nanosoc_eth_chiplet_pads"

// ---- outputs (relative -> they land in whatever directory you run from) -----
DRC RESULTS DATABASE "nanosoc_eth_chiplet_pads.drc.results" ASCII
DRC SUMMARY REPORT   "nanosoc_eth_chiplet_pads.drc.summary" REPLACE HIER
DRC CELL NAME YES CELL SPACE XFORM

// Cap results per check. A first full-chip run on a design that has never been
// through signoff DRC can generate millions of results (density and sealring
// checks especially) and an unbounded ASCII database will fill the disk.
// Raise to ALL only once the count is known to be sane.
DRC MAXIMUM RESULTS 1000
DRC MAXIMUM VERTEX  4096

// ---- process option: WE ARE LP, AND THE DECK DOES NOT ASSUME IT --------------
// The foundry deck's LP switch is COMMENTED OUT in the shipped file, and the
// deck never defines it itself. Without it the deck checks the wrong DPO.W.1 /
// DPO.S.1/2/3 variants and skips the LP high-voltage device block entirely.
// This design is CLN65LP throughout - tcbn65lp std cells, tphn65lpgv2od3_sl IO.
// Defining it HERE, before the INCLUDE, is legal and leaves the read-only
// foundry deck untouched.
#DEFINE LP

// ---- the rest of the mini@sic switch set ------------------------------------
// The manual's worked example is a different node; the below is the 65nm
// equivalent, CHECKED AGAINST THIS DECK rather than transcribed. Switch NAMES
// are named because you cannot operate the deck without them; where each one
// sits in the vendor file, and what the vendor's own comment says about it, are
// not reproduced -- read them in your own PDK.
//
// ALREADY CORRECT, no action: the full-chip switch is on in the shipped deck.
// Without it you do not see floating gates or CSR violations at all.
//
// DEFINE_PAD_BY_TEXT   <-- DO NOT ENABLE. Reverted 2026-08-10.
// The mini@sic manual offers it when you only have bondpad abstracts, and
// that IS our situation - but it is actively harmful here. Our top cell
// carries only 48 text labels, all on one metal layer, all signal names:
// there is NO VDD/VSS text anywhere and the deck's pad-text variable is still
// its placeholder. So every *_BY_TEXT layer resolves EMPTY, and ESD.1g swaps
// its exclusion basis to "devices on the VSS net as identified by text" -
// i.e. to nothing. Measured on this stream:
//     off : 2,509 results, 0 capped, LUP.* true count 0
//     ON  : >=8,509 results, 6 rulechecks CAPPED, LUP.* true count 222,350
// It manufactures a quarter of a million false latch-up violations. It only
// becomes usable once the pads carry real VDD/VSS text and the deck's pad-text
// variable names them. Off by default in the shipped deck.
//
// Pads are otherwise recognised by the passivation-opening layer, which appears
// NOWHERE in our stream, because it is absent from tpbn65v's LEF and
// -output_macros can only emit what the LEF contains. Without this, pad
// recognition is broken, which silently disarms the latch-up checks and stops
// the AP.W.2 "not inside the pad-opening layers" exemption from ever firing.
//
// CANNOT BE SET FROM THIS WRAPPER — both need a project copy of the deck header:
//   The chip-window switch, together with the four window-bound variables. The
//     deck declares those four as VARIABLE UNCONDITIONALLY and SVRF rejects a
//     VARIABLE redefinition, so defining the switch alone would pin the window
//     at the deck's default extent — wrong for this die. Until it is set the
//     deck derives the chip boundary from DRAWN METAL, so every boundary-derived
//     check (CSR, seal ring, density) measures against a synthetic outline. That
//     is the cause of the "entangled" CSR/density numbers in the 2026-08-10
//     counterfactual.
//   The WLCSP seal-ring switch is ON in the shipped deck and mini@sic is WIRE
//     BOND only. SVRF has no #UNDEFINE, so it cannot be cleared from here. Every
//     CSR.* and AP.R.1 number derives from the WLCSP branch until it is.
//
// NOT AVAILABLE AT 65nm, despite the manual's example at another node:
//   The floating-gate-by-text switch does not exist in this deck (grepped,
//   absent). The manual offers it to suppress false PO.R.19 floating-gate errors
//   when you only have abstracts; at 65nm the equivalent rule is PO.R.8 and there
//   is no such switch. Our 691 PO.R.8 results cannot be configured away — they
//   resolve only when imec replaces the abstract cells with real layout.

// ---- the foundry deck, unmodified -------------------------------------------
// Resolved per site, never named here: $DRC_FOUNDRY_DECK must select the deck
// for the SAME metal stack as the Innovus GdsOutMap used by write_stream. The
// metal option is fixed for this tapeout and a silently-wrong deck is worse than
// an edit; if the stack ever changes, change the site config AND the map file
// passed to write_stream together. run_drc.sh expands and readability-checks
// this before launching, so a missing or wrong value is a sentence, not an SVRF
// error 900k lines in.
//
// NOT FIXABLE FROM HERE: the shipped deck also has the WLCSP seal-ring switch
// active, and we are WIRE BOND. That selects the WLCSP seal-ring edge branch
// rather than the wire-bond branch, so the CSR.* corner geometry may be derived
// from degenerate inputs. SVRF has no #UNDEFINE, so undoing it needs a project
// copy of the deck header - do that only once the broker has confirmed their own
// switch set.
INCLUDE "$DRC_FOUNDRY_DECK"
